research/audit/wave7-certifier-tasks/ex-abel-dini-pair-for-the-harmonic-series.md-168-produces something that diverges more slowly still. Dividing by the square of the
research/audit/wave7-certifier-tasks/ex-abel-dini-pair-for-the-harmonic-series.md-169-running total overshoots into convergence. So exponent $1$ gives a divergent
research/audit/wave7-certifier-tasks/ex-abel-dini-pair-for-the-harmonic-series.md-170-member and exponent $2$ a convergent one. The absence of a slowest divergent
research/audit/wave7-certifier-tasks/ex-abel-dini-pair-for-the-harmonic-series.md:171:positive series comes from applying Abel-Dini again to the newly produced
research/audit/wave7-certifier-tasks/ex-abel-dini-pair-for-the-harmonic-series.md-172-divergent series, not from a last-exponent claim about this fixed pair.
