research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-1857-3.7 $(f \cdot 1_K)(k) = \sum_{i+j=k} f(i) 1_K(j)$ has at most one nonzero term, the one with $j = 0$ and $i = k$, so $(f \cdot 1_K)(k) = f(k)$ and $f \cdot 1_K = f$; moreover $1_K(0) = 1 \ne 0 = 0_K(0)$, so $1_K \ne 0_K$. [step 2.1, L1, L5]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-1858-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md:1859:4.1 Addition on $K$ is defined index by index, and $K$ is closed under it and under negation by [step 3.1]; so associativity, commutativity, the law $f + 0_K = f$ and the law $f + (-f) = 0_K$ each hold at every index by the corresponding law in $\mathbb{R}$, and $(K, +, 0_K)$ is an abelian group. [step 3.1, L1, L5]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-1860-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md:1861:5.1 By [step 4.1] addition makes $K$ an abelian group, by [step 3.2], [step 3.3] and [step 3.7] multiplication is commutative and associative with identity $1_K \ne 0_K$, and by [step 3.4] it distributes over addition; hence $K$ is a commutative ring with identity. [step 3.2, step 3.3, step 3.4, step 3.7, step 4.1]
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-1862-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-1863-6.1 Clause 1 is [step 2.1], clause 2 is [step 3.1] with [step 2.1], clause 3 is [step 5.1], clause 4 is [step 3.5] and [step 3.6], and clause 5 is [step 1.1] applied to $S = \operatorname{supp} f$, which is nonempty when $f \ne 0_K$ and bounded below because $f \in K$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1, step 3.5, step 3.6, step 5.1] ∎
