research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/thm-abel-dini--2ec0d3834efb2fd5.prompt.md-1423-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/thm-abel-dini--2ec0d3834efb2fd5.prompt.md-1424-1.2 Since $\sum b_k$ diverges and its terms are nonnegative, its exclusive partial sums $s_n = \sum_{k<n} b_k$ diverge to $+\infty$; and $B_n = s_{n+1}$, so $B_n \to +\infty$ as well, any index bound for $(s_n)$ serving for $(B_n)$. [given, L1, algebra]
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/thm-abel-dini--2ec0d3834efb2fd5.prompt.md-1425-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/thm-abel-dini--2ec0d3834efb2fd5.prompt.md:1426:2.1 Define $c_n := b_n / B_n$ for $n \in \mathbb{N}$. Each $c_n$ is positive, and by Abel-Dini applied to $(b_k)$ the series $\sum c_n$ diverges. [step 1.1, L2, L3]
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/thm-abel-dini--2ec0d3834efb2fd5.prompt.md-1427-
research/audit/wave7-preserved-refuters/thm-abel-dini--2ec0d3834efb2fd5.prompt.md-1428-2.2 Since $B_n \to +\infty$ there is $N \in \mathbb{N}$ with $B_n > 1$ for every $n \ge N$; for such $n$, $c_n = b_n/B_n < b_n$. [step 1.2, L3, choose]
