items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-1----
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-2-id: lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-3-kind: lemma
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:4:title: "The Prüfer decoding procedure is well defined and produces a labelled tree"
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-5-status: published
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-6-origin: session
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-7-provenance:
--
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-25-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-26-## Statement
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-27-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:28:Let $S\subseteq\mathbb N$ be finite with $|S|=n\ge2$, and let $(a_i)_{i<n-2}$ be a word on the label set $S$. The following procedure is well defined and produces a tree on $S$:
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-29-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:30:At stage $i$, choose the least unused label absent from the remaining word $a_i\cdots a_{n-3}$, join it to $a_i$, and mark it used. After all symbols are processed, join the two unused labels.
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-31-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-32-## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-33-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-34-**Given:** A word $(a_i)_{i<n-2}$ on a finite set $S\subseteq\mathbb N$ of cardinality $n\ge2$ ([[def-finite-cardinality]]).
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-35-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:36:[L1] A word with $r$ positions cannot contain more than $r$ distinct labels. Thus among $r+2$ available labels at least two are absent; every nonempty set of natural labels has a least element ([[lem-pigeonhole]], [[thm-well-ordering-principle]]).
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-37-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-38-[F1] A tree is a connected acyclic graph ([[def-tree-forest-and-leaf]]).
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-39-
--
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-43-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-44-**Proof technique:** induction on the stage number.
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-45-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:46:1.1 Before any word entry is processed, the graph has $n$ singleton components indexed by $S$, each containing exactly one unused label. [base]
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-47-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:48:1.2 Assume that after $i$ stages the built edges form a forest and every component contains exactly one unused label. [ih]
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-49-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:50:2.1 There are $n-i$ unused labels and $n-2-i$ entries in the remaining word. Hence at least two unused labels are absent from that word, so the least such label $v$ exists. Every label used at an earlier stage was absent from the then-remaining word and hence from its current suffix, so $a_i$ is unused; because $a_i$ occurs in the remaining word, $v\ne a_i$. [step 1.2, L1]
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-51-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:52:3.1 The label $a_i$ lies in a component different from the component containing $v$: by the induction hypothesis each component contains exactly one unused label, and $a_i\ne v$. Adding $va_i$ and marking $v$ used joins those components without a cycle and leaves exactly one unused label in the new component. Thus the invariant holds after stage $i+1$. [step 1.2, step 2.1]
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-53-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:54:4.1 Induction gives the invariant after all $n-2$ word stages. Exactly two unused labels and two components remain; joining those labels makes the graph connected without creating a cycle. [step 3.1, L2]
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md-55-
items/lem-prufer-decoding-produces-a-tree.md:56:5.1 The output uses all labels in $S$ and is connected and acyclic, hence is a labelled tree on $S$. [step 4.1, F1, discharge-induction] ∎
--
