items/cex-power-of-a-product-in-a-nonabelian-group.md-78-
items/cex-power-of-a-product-in-a-nonabelian-group.md-79-- **Why this matters for the library.** The published [[lem-power-laws]] states $(ab)^{n} = a^{n}b^{n}$ with no commuting hypothesis and is correct, because it is a statement about a field, where multiplication is commutative by definition. Carrying that form over to groups would be a false generalisation, and this item is the witness.
items/cex-power-of-a-product-in-a-nonabelian-group.md-80-
items/cex-power-of-a-product-in-a-nonabelian-group.md:81:- The same pair $g, h$ witnesses that $\operatorname{Sym}(\{1,2,3\})$ is not abelian ([[ex-symmetric-group-on-three-letters]]), which is no accident. At $n = 2$ the equation $(gh)^{2} = g^{2}h^{2}$ reads $ghgh = gghh$, and cancelling $g$ on the left and $h$ on the right ([[lem-group-cancellation]]) turns it into $hg = gh$; so at this exponent the law holds for a pair exactly when the pair commutes, and every non-abelian group supplies a counterexample.
--
