items/ex-integers-mod-n-additive-group.md-90-1.5 In the group $\mathbb{Z}/n$ the $k$-th power of $[1]$ in additive notation is $[\iota(k)]$ for every $k \in \mathbb{N}$: the set of $k$ for which this holds contains $0$, since the identity is $[0] = [\iota(0)]$, and is closed under $\sigma$, since the power at $\sigma(k)$ is the power at $k$ plus $[1]$, that is $[\iota(k)] + [1] = [\iota(k) + 1] = [\iota(\sigma(k))]$. [L1, L2, L6, L7]
items/ex-integers-mod-n-additive-group.md-91-
items/ex-integers-mod-n-additive-group.md:92:2.1 $(\mathbb{Z}/n, +, [0])$ is an abelian group: associativity, commutativity, the identity law $[a] + [0] = [a+0] = [a]$ and the inverse law $[a] + [-a] = [0]$ all follow from the corresponding identities in $\mathbb{Z}$ applied to representatives, which is legitimate by step 1.2. [step 1.2, L1, L5]
items/ex-integers-mod-n-additive-group.md-93-
items/ex-integers-mod-n-additive-group.md-94-2.2 Every class has a representative $r$ with $0 \le r < \bar n$: given $a$, divide $a = q\bar n + r$ with $0 \le r < \bar n$, so $a - r = q\bar n$ and $[a] = [r]$. Moreover $r \ge 0$ gives $r = \iota(s)$ for a unique $s \in \mathbb{N}$, and $\iota(s) < \iota(n)$ gives $s < n$. [step 1.3, L2, L3, L4]
