items/thm-quotient-abelian-iff-contains-commutator-subgroup.md:51:1.1 Suppose $G/N$ is abelian. For $g,h\in G$, the commutator of the cosets $gN$ and $hN$ is the identity, so [L1] gives $[g,h]N=N$; hence $[g,h]\in N$ by [L3]. [given, L1, L3, F2, algebra]
items/thm-quotient-abelian-iff-contains-commutator-subgroup.md-52-
items/thm-quotient-abelian-iff-contains-commutator-subgroup.md:53:1.2 Conversely, suppose $[G,G]\subseteq N$. Then for any $g,h\in G$, one has $[g,h]\in N$, so [L3] and [L1] show that the commutator of $gN$ and $hN$ is $N$. Multiplying the equality $(gN)(hN)(gN)^{-1}(hN)^{-1}=N$ on the right by $(hN)(gN)$ gives $(gN)(hN)=(hN)(gN)$. Thus $G/N$ is abelian. [given, L1, L3, F2, algebra]
items/thm-quotient-abelian-iff-contains-commutator-subgroup.md-54-
items/thm-quotient-abelian-iff-contains-commutator-subgroup.md-55-2.1 The subgroup $N$ contains every commutator, so it contains the subgroup they generate: $[G,G]\subseteq N$. [step 1.1, F1, L2]
