items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-51-
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-52-**Given:** A field $F$ with operations $+$ and $\cdot$ and distinguished elements $0 \ne 1$, satisfying the axioms (A), (M) and (D) of [[def-field]].
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-53-
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md:54:[A1] Axiom (M) of [[def-field]]: multiplication is associative and commutative on **all** of $F$, and $x \cdot 1 = x$ for **every** $x \in F$, the element $0$ included; moreover $(F \setminus \{0\}, \cdot)$ is an abelian group with identity $1$, so every $x \ne 0$ has a multiplicative inverse $x^{-1}$ with $x \cdot x^{-1} = 1$.
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-55-
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md:56:[A2] Axiom (A): $(F,+)$ is an abelian group with identity $0$; addition is associative and commutative, $x + 0 = x$ for all $x$, and every $x$ has an additive inverse $-x$ with $x + (-x) = 0$ ([[def-field]], [[def-group]]).
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-57-
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-58-[A3] Axiom (D), left distributivity: $x(y+z) = xy + xz$ for all $x, y, z \in F$ ([[def-field]]).
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-59-
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-60-[A4] $0 \ne 1$ ([[def-field]]).
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-61-
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md:62:[L1] A ring is an abelian group under addition, a monoid under multiplication, and satisfies both distributive laws; it is commutative when its multiplication is ([[def-ring]], [[def-commutative-ring]], [[def-semigroup-and-monoid]]).
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-63-
items/lem-field-is-a-commutative-ring.md-64-[L2] In a field, $ab = 0$ implies $a = 0$ or $b = 0$ ([[lem-of-no-zero-divisors]]).
