items/ex-additive-and-multiplicative-groups-of-a-field.md:68:1.3 Every $x \in F$ is invertible for $+$: $x + (-x) = 0$ by (A) and $(-x) + x = 0$ by commutativity, so $-x$ is a two-sided inverse. Hence $(F,+,0)$ is an abelian group. [L1, L4]
items/ex-additive-and-multiplicative-groups-of-a-field.md-69-
items/ex-additive-and-multiplicative-groups-of-a-field.md-70-1.4 For every $z \in F$, $z \cdot 0 = 0$: by (D), $z \cdot 0 = z(0 + 0) = z \cdot 0 + z \cdot 0$, and adding $-(z \cdot 0)$ to both sides gives $0 = z \cdot 0$. [L1, L3]
